hello everyone thanks for joining me in  this video today I'd like to go over  ways to practice trills things to keep  in mind and also share with you an  exercise that will allow you to practice  chills in a concentrated manner in the  context of some pretty harmonies as well  now if you'd like to follow along with  the sheet music you can download it for  free from my website something to keep  in mind is that there are many many  different types of trills in different  lengths different dynamics different  Tambor's depending on the context of the  music here are some examples  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you  you  you  [Music]  one of the things that really helps me  when learning a trill is to actually  write it out as if it wasn't a trill at  all now in practice we're not going to  be playing these trills in such a  measured way but this definitely helps  us visualize the notes that go into the  trill so here I want to put in a little  ornament  so let's actually write that out so if  we slow it way down you can find out the  note value that we're gonna use to write  it out so these will be 32nd note  triplets so the main reason I do this is  because when I see the notes written out  visually it helps me attend to every  single note of the trill similarly when  you have a long trill maybe you want to  actually plan it out just to see how it  feels with triplets or groups of four  groups of five however you want to  organize it so for example let's take  this trill instead of just treating it  as one long held note with a trill let's  break it up so this is my eighth note  pulse the note values will be in groups  of six per eighth note so even if we're  going to abandon this framework when  we're actually playing the trill it  helps to sort of form your trill in the  first place another thing to keep in  mind is fingering now there are many  different types of trills so there are  many different types of fingerings but  let's go over just a few of them I think  the most commonly used fingers for  trills our fingers one two and three so  one and three which is actually my least  favorite fingering and we'll get back to  why two three and one three one two one  alternating  so depending on the context sometimes I  need that sort of power where I need to  be alternating between fingers but  sometimes I don't and sometimes I can't  maybe it's a trill underneath the left  hand or vice versa then I don't have  that space to be changing around so  beyond these three fingers I also really  like fingers to four also three four and  if I need a little more sound I'll use  four to three to also 3/5 and 4/5 now  that's a little weak anyway those  fingerings also apply to the left hand  but in Reverse so now let's move on to  talking a little bit about the mechanics  of playing the trills so we're going  back and forth between two notes or two  sets of notes so let's imagine we have a  balance point in the middle and we have  a firm object here which our fingers  will be balancing on and we're gonna go  back and forth like this so this motion  to me represents the motion that we have  to keep in mind for a trill so it's more  than just going back and forth between  two notes and hitting those two notes  it's this sort of teeter-totter motion  and that's where you can get the speed  and to keep this very fast and very  consistent the amount of distance your  fingers go from playing the keys and  going back up based on this motion  should always be consistent and that  will also translate into a consistent  and controlled sound now when we're  practicing we can keep this in mind and  pay close attention to how far our  fingers are moving up from the keys and  how in sync the two fingers are with  each other  so kind of similar to writing out the  notes of the trill just visualizing this  lever and this middle point when I'm  playing a trill helps me because I'm  visualizing this movement and this sense  of balance that centers my trills now  let's go back to this trill and we're  going to be building up in speed let's  first start with groups of two  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  approximately some more times than not  it's much more reliable to be using the  fingers rather than the wrists because  it's very easy to try and play all kinds  of trills with just this wrist motion  because you kind of just need to shake  but you'll find that in most cases doing  a trill like this will give you so much  control in sound and clarity but that  being said there are some instances that  you probably are better off using this  wrist rotation type of trill  all right so here is an exercise I came  up with to practice drills in a somewhat  isolated manner while also sort of  working on transposition as well so here  are the notes we're going to be trilling  on this see  so now we can alter this in many ways we  can use different fingerings to for  [Music]  let's increase the volume and do four to  three to  we can also use this to practice  transitioning from one trill to the next  and we're going to be changing the  fingerings to do so but depending on if  we're going from a white key to a white  key or white key to black black to black  etc it'll change just as an example for  a fingering for such transition let's go  from C to F and we'll start with 1 &amp; 2  and when we go to the F will first start  with 3 but we will do 3 4 to 3 and then  we're in a prime position to use 2 &amp; 3  so we can also modify this to play  shorter trills so we're going to take  this note and turn it into triplets and  we're going to be adding 3 notes to the  main note starting from the top now in  order to go through all the different  fingerings we're going to be changing  from 2 1 3 1 3 2 for our first set and  then 2 4 3 4 3 5 for our next set 4 5 3  5 3 4 for the next set and for the final  set 2 4 2 3 1 3 and we're back to the  beginning  so hopefully you can take this and  modify it to your liking depending on  the type of trill that you'd like to  work on anyway I hope you found that  helpful thanks again for joining me in  this video and I'll see you in the next  one thanks  [Music]
 

